PROPOSED patch, NOT applied to src/. This is the deliverable's integration
illustration: a unified diff against src/display/ui/default/SleepAnimation.cpp
at HEAD (branch idf5) showing exactly what adding the PIE-vectorized
blendRow composite would look like, plus this rationale. Apply with
`git apply` (or `patch -p1`) from the repo root if and when someone with
device/QEMU access decides to try it -- it has NOT been applied, built,
flashed, or run anywhere as part of this work. See
tools/animbench/kernels-blend/ for the correctness proofs this patch's
content is drawn from (blendGroup8General's asm block is verified
byte-for-byte identical, mnemonic-for-mnemonic, to the standalone
blend_group8.S file that was assembled and instruction-counted with the
real toolchain -- see prove_asm_match.py -- and the same block was proven
to compile+assemble cleanly as part of blend_pie_kernel.cpp via
build_and_report.sh, and separately confirmed to compile+assemble cleanly
in exactly this patch's textual form via a standalone syntax check before
this file was written).

Rationale
---------
blendRow (the per-row alpha-composite pass, SleepAnimation.cpp:350-370) is
measured at ~7.9ms/frame median on device, ~25% of the core-0 render
budget, running unconditionally scalar. This patch adds a second code path,
blendRowPie, that processes 8-pixel-aligned groups within each run: an
all-opaque group (every lane a==255) copies via one EE.VLD.128/EE.VST.128
pair (no arithmetic, matching the existing precedent in
tools/overlaybench/kernels/overlay_blend.cpp's blendRow_pie_asm); a group
with no opaque lanes runs the new blendGroup8General vector arithmetic
(the piece that file's own header comment explicitly declined to write,
judging the hand-scheduled SAR-juggling sequence "too likely to ship a
silently-wrong result... without a way to execute it" -- this patch is
that missing piece, now proven three ways: host bit-exactness for both the
per-pixel formula (23.2M cases) and the whole group-dispatch function
(50,000 randomized rows), plus a semantic-trace interpreter executing the
literal instruction sequence (1.6M lane-checks) -- see the report this
patch accompanies for exactly what each proof does and does not establish);
a mixed group (some lanes opaque, some not) falls back to the same scalar
body blendRow already uses, since no group-level vector shortcut is safe
across a mix (the a==255 counterexample in the comment above
kBlendGroupConsts is why). Pixels before/after the first/last 8-pixel-
aligned boundary within a run (a run's own start/end need not be aligned)
use that same scalar fallback too.

What this patch does NOT do: change blendRow itself (left in place,
unused after the call-site swap, so it stays available as an immediate
revert -- just swap the call site back); change scrimRow/scrimRowPie/
scale565Oct or anything else in the file; touch the two-pass ordering,
the scrim-skip gating, or any other part of renderFrame's band loop
besides the one blendRow(...) call.

UPDATE (2026-08-31, after this patch was first written): blendGroup8General
has now executed under Espressif's qemu-system-xtensa fork, not just been
proven on the host. tools/qemubench/tests/blend_row/main.c transcribes this
exact instruction sequence (mnemonics, operand registers, ssai immediates,
load/store order all unchanged from blendGroup8General above -- see that
file's own header for why it's a transcription, not a regeneration) and
runs it against the "general_all_nonopaque" case from
tools/animbench/kernels-blend/blend_qemu_vectors.h -- eight lanes, every
channel's arithmetic exercised, several boundary alphas in one group.
`./tools/qemubench/build.sh tests/blend_row && ./tools/qemubench/run.sh
build/tests/blend_row.elf` (bare-metal, no pio, no physical device --
tools/qemubench/build.sh's own header explains why it's exempt from this
effort's offline-only constraint): RESULT: PASS, all 8 lanes bit-exact
against the host-proven expected output. This is real ssai/SAR-threading
and EE.VADDS.S16 execution, on the specific ISA implementation this
project already trusts for other PIE code (scale565Oct, GM_PIETEST) --
not a claim about physical silicon, since this is an emulator, and not a
claim about the full 23.2M-case host sweep (only this one representative
group ran under QEMU; the exhaustive coverage is host-side only). The two
risks named below are accordingly downgraded from "unchecked" to "checked
once, on a representative case, under emulation" -- worth a broader QEMU
sweep or a real-device run before this ships unconditionally, but no
longer a completely open question.

Open risks before this should ship (see the report's honest-assessment
section for the full list, and the UPDATE above): the semantic-trace
interpreter models the documented instruction semantics, and the QEMU run
above confirms that model against this specific fork's actual
implementation for one representative case -- neither is a claim about
real silicon, which could still differ (a hardware erratum QEMU's model
doesn't reproduce). The saturating-add bound (EE.VADDS.S16 clamps at
+-32767; every sum this design produces tops out at 16128, so saturation
is argued never to trigger, and the QEMU case above exercises alphas at
1/2/127/128/253/254 without hitting it) and the SAR-threading across nine
`ssai` calls per group (now QEMU-confirmed correct for this one case) are
the two things worth broadening coverage on before a real-device run.

